Chapter: PlugRealEdge
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The Real edges, printed as IEEE-754 patterns because `show` on a Real is not
 available on both plugs. real-to-int is the interesting one: bare metal emits
 cvttsd2si, which answers INT64_MIN for a NaN, an infinity, or an out-of-range
 truncation, and neither target's instruction does that unaided.

 `==` ON A REAL IS REFUSED BY THE COMPILER -- CDX2085, "floating-point equality
 is not safe, use the ~ operator" -- which is worth recording because it means
 the plugs' IrEq arm for a Real is unreachable from ordinary source. It is
 reached through `~`, and through a record's generated equality.

 The values are BUILT rather than written. Codex has no exponent literal, and a
 constant expression would in any case be a fair target for fold-constants --
 which would move the arithmetic out of the emitter and into the compiler, where
 this probe cannot see it.

 We say:

Section: Entry

  sq : Real -> Real
  sq (x) = x * x

  zero : Integer -> Real
  zero (n) = real-from-int (n - n)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("to-int 2.7    = " & show (real-to-int 2.7))
    print-line-uni ("to-int -2.7   = " & show (real-to-int (0.0 - 2.7)))
    print-line-uni ("to-int 2^64   = " & show (real-to-int (sq (sq (sq (sq (sq (sq 2.0)))))))) 
    print-line-uni ("to-int -2^64  = " & show (real-to-int (0.0 - sq (sq (sq (sq (sq (sq 2.0))))))))
    print-line-uni ("from-int -5   = " & show (real-to-bits (real-from-int (0 - 5))))
    print-line-uni ("1/0 bits      = " & show (real-to-bits (1.0 / zero 3)))
    print-line-uni ("-1/0 bits     = " & show (real-to-bits ((0.0 - 1.0) / zero 3)))
    print-line-uni ("to-int 1/0    = " & show (real-to-int (1.0 / zero 3)))
    print-line-uni ("nan < 1       = " & show (if (zero 3 / zero 3) < 1.0 then 1 else 0))
    print-line-uni ("nan >= 1      = " & show (if (zero 3 / zero 3) >= 1.0 then 1 else 0))
    print-line-uni ("to-int 0/0    = " & show (real-to-int (zero 3 / zero 3)))
    print-line-uni ("-0.0 ~0 0.0   = " & show (if (0.0 - 0.0) ~0 0.0 then 1 else 0))
    print-line-uni ("-0.0 bits     = " & show (real-to-bits (0.0 - 0.0)))
    print-line-uni ("-2.0 < -1.0   = " & show (if (0.0 - 2.0) < (0.0 - 1.0) then 1 else 0))
  end
